{
  "dom_id": "step-q113878",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14223546/assets/8b242c0012bdbc048f350158333b78e9484d716aa44f28511df2d7126dd02098.png)\n\nFor the function $f(x)=\\frac{4x}{1+{x}^{2}}$ shown above, what is the expression for the area $A$ as a definite integral?",
  "worked_solution": "We want to express the area $A$ between the function $f(x)=\\frac{4x}{1+{x}^{2}}$ and the $x$-axis, between $x=0$ and $x=3.$\n\nThis can be expressed as the definite integral of $f(x)=\\frac{4x}{1+{x}^{2}}$ between the lower limit $x=0$ and the upper limit $x=3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{4x}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$",
